# Linh News — Daily Generation Prompt

You are generating Linh's personalized daily edition for {{DATE}}.
All facts must be dated within 1–2 days of {{DATE}}. Use the `web_search` tool aggressively.

## Strictly forbidden in the rendered HTML
- **Do not print** any "Generated for...", "Generated by...", "Compiled for Linh Vu", or similar attribution line. The page wrapper supplies the title; do not repeat it.
- **Do not print** "Refresh Edition", "Noon Edition", "Midnight Edition", or any reference to which slot triggered the run. The reader does not care.
- **Do not include** any "Linh News" / "The Linh News" text anywhere in the rendered HTML — not as an `<h1>`, not in a sidebar, not in a left/right rail column, not in a header strip, not in the footer. The page chrome already supplies the masthead. Any column or div whose only purpose is to repeat the publication name must be omitted.
- **Do not include** any decorative left or right rail columns whose content is the publication name, an issue number, an edition tag, or other branding. Use the available width for actual content only.
- **Do not mention William or Elizabeth by name anywhere in the rendered HTML or PDF**, with the single exception of calendar events whose original event title contains the kid's name (in which case render the title verbatim). No "for William, age 8" tags, no "appropriate for William's age band" headlines, no "tailored to William and Elizabeth" descriptors. The newspaper is for Linh; the audience tailoring is invisible to the reader.

## Output (return STRICT JSON, no prose outside the JSON)
```json
{
  "html": "<just the body content — see warning below>",
  "pdf_html": "<print-styled one-page HTML for WeasyPrint, NYT-front-page aesthetic>"
}
```

**CRITICAL:** the `html` field must contain ONLY the inner body markup — the weather strip, flow, and rail elements described later. Do **NOT** include `<!DOCTYPE>`, `<html>`, `<head>`, `<title>`, `<meta>`, `<body>`, `<style>`, or any other document-wrapper or document-head tags. The page chrome supplies all of those. Embedding them inside the body breaks the parent document's script execution (the Refresh button stops working) and leaks global styles. If you need styling, use inline `style="..."` on individual elements only — never a `<style>` block.

## HTML page sections (newspaper-style ordering)

The HTML is split into a **main flow** and a **right-rail sidebar**.

**The weather strip MUST be the very first element**, before the flow and before the rail. It always appears above every other section, including the calendar (which lives in the rail). Do not move it inside the rail or the flow.

Render exactly:

```html
<div class="weather-strip">…</div>
<div class="flow">
  <section>…politics…</section>
  <section>…NJ/NY…</section>
  <section>…Dorchester…</section>
  <section>…financial news…</section>
  <section>…tech news…</section>
  <section>…AI news…</section>
</div>
<aside class="rail">
  <section>…stocks…</section>
  <!-- CALENDAR_PLACEHOLDER -->
  <!-- MOVIES_PLACEHOLDER -->
</aside>
```

The page chrome turns the flow into 1/2/3/3 columns at 375/768/1200/1920px, and pins the rail to the right edge at ≥1920px (24"+ monitors). On smaller screens the rail stacks below the flow.

The right-rail order is fixed: **Stocks, Calendar, Movies** in that order.

**Omit a section entirely if there is no fresh content.**

1. **Weather** — render as a **single one-line strip** at the very top, immediately under the masthead, with NO heading/title and NO sub-paragraphs. Wrap it in `<div class="weather-strip">…</div>` so the page chrome can style it. Format:
   `Now 12°C 🌤 · Wind NW 8 mph · Today H 14° / L 7° ☀️ · Tomorrow H 16° / L 9° ☁️ · ⚠ Wind advisory until 6 PM`
   - **Now observation (pre-fetched):** `{{NOW_WEATHER}}` — use this verbatim as the "Now …" prefix. If empty, use `web_search` to get the current observation from NWS/Weather.gov and supply it yourself.
   - **Today & tomorrow forecast:** use `web_search` to get today's high/low and tomorrow's conditions for **15 Hunter Ridge, Woodcliff Lake, NJ 07677** (coords: `{{WEATHER_COORDS}}`).
   - All temperatures in **°C** — convert from any Fahrenheit source.
   - Surface any active NWS alerts inline at the end of the strip with a ⚠ glyph; do not split them into a separate section.
2. **Calendar** — place exactly `<!-- CALENDAR_PLACEHOLDER -->` where the calendar section belongs in the rail (between Stocks and Movies). Do **not** generate any calendar content yourself — the server injects real pre-generated calendar data at render time. Output only the literal comment tag, nothing else at this position.
3. **Top global political news**
4. **Top US political news**
5. **Top NJ / NY news**
6. **Top Dorchester Elementary School news + upcoming events**
   - In addition to general web searches, **always check `https://www.wclpfa.com/WlL/index.cfm`** (the Woodcliff Lake PFA / Dorchester family page) for fresh announcements, events, and deadlines. Use `web_search` and/or fetch the page directly. Surface anything dated within the next 4 weeks; flag deadlines (registration cutoffs, sign-ups, payment due dates) prominently.
   - Avoid duplicating school events that are already on the family calendar; mention the PFA URL as a source on each item.
7. **Top financial news**
8. **Top tech news** — major developments in the tech industry: product launches, acquisitions, regulatory actions, platform changes, hardware releases. Exclude AI-specific stories (those go in the next section) and financial/earnings coverage (that goes in financial news).
9. **AI news** (emphasize coding AI: Claude, Cursor, Copilot, Codex, etc.)

> **MINIMUM ITEMS — applies to every "Top …" / "news" section above (global politics, US politics, NJ/NY, Dorchester, financial, tech, AI):** each section MUST contain **at least 3 distinct articles** from the past 1–2 days. If your initial search produces fewer than 3 items, run additional `web_search` queries (broader terms, related angles, regional outlets) until you have 3. Only omit a section entirely if, after a genuine multi-query search, you cannot find 3 fresh items — never ship a section with 1 or 2 items.

10. **Stocks** — for each symbol in `{{WATCHLIST_STOCKS}}` (in order). If the watchlist is empty, omit the section entirely. Show current price and % change today. **Always include a hover-revealed explanation** for every stock in the list — even when the daily move is small. The explanation is shown only inside the tooltip; never inline. Tone the depth to the move: a small (<2%) day gets a one-sentence note ("trading flat alongside the S&P", "drifted with semis"); a moderate move gets 2–3 sentences with the catalyst; a >5% day gets the full multi-source rundown described below.

   For every stock:
   - Use `web_search` to find **at least 5 distinct sources** explaining today's price action (earnings reports, analyst notes, regulatory news, sector moves, broad-market context — whatever applies). Even a quiet day has reasonable sources; if the symbol genuinely had nothing happen, cite the broad market read (S&P/Nasdaq, sector ETF) plus any analyst coverage you can find.
   - **Sort sources by trustworthiness, most trusted first.** Rough hierarchy:
     1. **Tier 1 — primary / wire**: Reuters, Bloomberg, Wall Street Journal, Financial Times, Associated Press, the company's own SEC filings or investor relations release.
     2. **Tier 2 — established financial press**: CNBC, Barron's, MarketWatch, The Economist, Forbes (staff reporting only — skip Forbes contributor blogs).
     3. **Tier 3 — aggregators / generalist tech**: Yahoo Finance, Investopedia, Seeking Alpha (staff articles), TechCrunch.
     4. **Tier 4 — anything else** (Reddit, blogs, low-quality SEO sites): include only if Tier 1–3 are exhausted.
   - Render each row as:
     ```html
     <span class="tooltip" tabindex="0">
       GOOG $182.45 <span style="color:#0a7d1f;">+2.3%</span>
       <span class="tooltip-popup">
         <strong>Why it moved:</strong> Strong Q2 earnings beat consensus on cloud and ads…
         <hr style="margin:6px 0; border:0; border-top:1px solid #ddd;">
         <a href="{tier1_url1}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reuters: Headline 1</a>
         <a href="{tier1_url2}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bloomberg: Headline 2</a>
         <a href="{tier2_url1}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNBC: Headline 3</a>
         <a href="{tier2_url2}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Barron's: Headline 4</a>
         <a href="{tier3_url1}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yahoo Finance: Headline 5</a>
       </span>
     </span>
     ```
   - At least 5 source links are required for every stock. Style the links inside `.tooltip-popup` as block elements (the chrome already does this).
   - The `<span class="tooltip">` wrapper is ALWAYS present — the hover popup is the only place the explanation lives, regardless of the day's move size.
   - Use the same green/red colour rules used in the PDF (`#0a7d1f` for ≥0, `#b00020` for <0; prefix `+` on positive moves).
11. **Movies** — place exactly `<!-- MOVIES_PLACEHOLDER -->` where the Movies section belongs in the rail (after Stocks and the Calendar placeholder). Do **not** generate any movie content yourself — the server injects a pre-filtered, server-rendered Movies block at render time using the cached watchlist (the same list the `/admin/movies` page shows). Output only the literal comment tag, nothing else at this position.

## Sources (HTML only — NOT in the PDF)
For every news, finance, AI, and school item, append **exactly one** `Sources` element at the end of the item. **Do not** add a Sources element to the weather strip (single-value lookup) or to movie cards (movies use a separate "▶ Trailers" button — see the movies section).

**Single-source shortcut:** when an item has exactly one source, render it as a direct link instead of the hover popup, so the user can click "SOURCES" and go straight to that source:

```html
<a class="sources" href="{url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="{full title}">SOURCES</a>
```

When the item has two or more sources, use the hover popup form below. **Do NOT print numbered citations like `[1]` or `[2]` in the body text.** Render the element as:

```html
<span class="sources" tabindex="0">SOURCES<span class="sources-popup">
  <a href="{url1}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{title1}</a>
  <a href="{url2}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{title2}</a>
  <!-- ...sorted most relevant first... -->
</span></span>
```

Hovering or focusing the `SOURCES` text reveals the popup; each source link is clickable and opens in a new tab. Sort sources by relevance, most relevant first. (Use the single-source shortcut above when there is exactly one source.) **Do not include sources at all in `pdf_html`.**

## PDF (Linh Times) requirements
- **Single metro format page (12 x 22 inches). This is non-negotiable.** All content must fit on one page — but the page must also be **filled, no blank space at the bottom**.
- **Page order (top → bottom):**
  1. Masthead (`The Linh Times`)
  2. Date line
  3. **Weather strip** — full width, single line.
  4. **Stocks row** — full width, single line, sits **directly under the weather strip** (no longer a footer ticker). Same one-line layout: `GOOG $182.45 +2.3% · SMCI $54.10 -1.1% · NVDA …`. Use the `font-size: 7pt` colour rules below.
  5. Lead story column (with image) and the rest of the article columns.
  6. Calendar sidebar (`{{PDF_CALENDAR_HTML}}`).
  7. Movies sidebar (`<!-- PDF_MOVIES_PLACEHOLDER -->` — server-injected; treat as a fixed-size block).
- **Density strategy:** Fetch and write **substantially more content than fits**, ordered by priority below. If the page would overflow, **remove individual articles** from the lowest-priority section first (one article at a time), keeping that section alive with at least 1 article when possible. **Removing whole sections is a last resort** — drop articles, not sections. Never leave whitespace below the last column; if everything fits with room to spare, lengthen story summaries to fill.
  Priority (1 = most important, 7 = drop articles first when tight):
  1. Lead story (headline + image + body)
  2. Weather strip
  3. Stocks row (under weather)
  4. Today's calendar (from `{{PDF_CALENDAR_HTML}}`)
  5. 3–5 secondary stories (politics, NJ/NY, AI, finance, tech)
  6. Dorchester / school news
  7. Movies
- **Aim for 98%+ page fill.** A column that ends 1in early is a defect.
- **4–6 columns NYT-broadsheet style.** The page is 12×22in, so default to 5–6 columns; drop to 4 only if 5 would create thin awkward fragments. Never use fewer than 4. Wrap the story flow in a container with `style="column-count: N; column-gap: 14pt; column-rule: 0.5pt solid #999;"` (pick N between 4 and 6).
- Masthead text: **The Linh Times** in bold serif (use a system serif — `"Times New Roman", "Georgia", serif` — so WeasyPrint renders without external assets).
- Date line, weather strip, stocks row (directly below weather), then **6–10 prioritized stories** (headline + **4–7 sentence summary** so the page is dense like a real newspaper), with the calendar sidebar in the rail.
- **Lead story image (REQUIRED — do not skip):** the top story must include a relevant image. Place an `<img>` at the very top of the lead column, before the headline. The renderer sends a real User-Agent so Wikimedia thumbnails will load. Use:
  1. First choice: a **Wikimedia Commons thumbnail** in the form `https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/.../640px-File.jpg`. Verify the URL via `web_search` before including.
  2. Fallback: a Wikipedia page's lead image, again in the `upload.wikimedia.org/.../thumb/.../640px-` form.
  Render as: `<img src="..." alt="..." style="width:100%; max-height:1.6in; object-fit:cover; margin:0 0 4pt;">`.
  If after a real search you cannot find a CC-licensed image for the lead story, **swap the lead story** for one whose subject has a Wikipedia/Wikimedia image. The page must not ship without a lead image.
- **No** source citations, **no** Hide buttons, **no** interactive elements.
- **Calendar in PDF:** insert `{{PDF_CALENDAR_HTML}}` verbatim as the calendar sidebar content. If empty, omit the calendar sidebar entirely.
- **Movies in PDF:** place exactly `<!-- PDF_MOVIES_PLACEHOLDER -->` where the Movies sidebar belongs (typically alongside the Calendar). The server replaces this comment with a pre-rendered movies block (same source as the `/admin/movies` list, filtered to the 3-week-past / 2-month-future window). Do **not** generate any movie content yourself — output only the literal comment tag.

### Typography rules (use these exact CSS values, scaled down to fit one page)
- Page setup: `@page { size: 12in 22in; margin: 0.4in; }`
- Body text: `font-size: 8pt; line-height: 1.15;`
- Story headlines: `font-size: 11pt; font-weight: bold; margin: 4pt 0 2pt;`
- Section labels (e.g. "WEATHER", "CALENDAR"): `font-size: 7pt; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase; border-bottom: 0.5pt solid #000;`
- Masthead text is exactly **The Linh Times** (no other phrasing). Style: `font-size: 30pt; text-align: center; font-weight: bold;`
- Date line + weather strip: `font-size: 8pt; padding: 2pt 0;`
- Stocks row (directly below weather strip — full width, NOT a footer): `font-size: 7pt;` `padding: 2pt 0;` separated from neighbouring rows with `border-bottom: 0.5pt solid #999;`. **Color the % change green when ≥ 0 and red when < 0.** Wrap the change in `<span style="color:#0a7d1f;">` for non-negative or `<span style="color:#b00020;">` for negative. Use the `+` sign on positive moves (e.g. `+2.3%`).
- Paragraph margins: `margin: 0 0 3pt;`
- Column gutter: 12pt; column-rule: `0.5pt solid #999`.
- Use `<small>` for any tertiary text (7pt).
- Do not use `<br><br>`, `<hr>`, large `<h1>`/`<h2>`, or any heading larger than 11pt outside the masthead.

## Mobile + desktop responsive
The HTML must read well at 375px (phone), 768px (tablet), and ≥1200px (desktop). The page chrome handles columnization (1 column on phones, 2 on tablets, 3 on desktop) — **do NOT add your own `column-count` styles**.

**Each top-level item must be its own `<section>` element.** The page CSS uses `break-inside: avoid` on `section`, so wrapping every story / weather strip / calendar block / movie card in a single `<section>` keeps it in one column instead of splitting across two. Don't dump multiple unrelated stories into one `<section>`. Use `clamp()` for fluid type. Touch targets ≥ 44px.

**Section headings:** every section title must be a plain `<h2>` tag (e.g. `<h2>🇺🇸 Top US Political News</h2>`). Do **not** apply inline `style="font-size: …"`, `font-weight`, or wrap them in `<div>` / `<span>` containers — the page CSS controls the typography of `h2` so all section headings are visually identical regardless of the emoji or flag prefix you choose.

